icProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Jaffa argues that original intent—grounded in natural law—should govern constitutional interpretation, and that Meese, Rehnquist, and Bork misconstrue original intent by rejecting natural law and the Declaration’s role.","confidence":0.66},"academicOrientation":{"score":43,"confidence":0.46},"profileConfidence":0.62,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Edwin Meese","confidence":0.62},{"name":"Chief Justice William Rehnquist","confidence":0.44},{"name":"Robert Bork","confidence":0.42}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"6bf70ed43e5efeb1be3881a188d3d4d321b33d21623fe2678644eed476f40bfb","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","secondarySubjects":["American History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily discusses constitutional interpretation and the role of original intent in jurisprudence, which aligns it closely with Politics & Government, while also engaging with historical debates around the Constitution in an American context.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"315e89e12a76f79a64f6c9fc83132a20ed715b682843a6771ba8dbbcf65f6ceb","summary":"Original Intent and the Framers of the Constitution: A Disputed Question is a unique contribution to the debate, begun by Attorney General Edwin Meese in the second Reagan administration, over the \"original intentions of the Framers.\" Professor Jaffa agrees entirely with Meese's opinion that there is a need to confine judges to interpreting, not making law. Jaffa also agrees that original intent, rightly understood, is the only sound basis of constitutional jurisprudence. But he contends that Meese, Chief Justice William Rehnquist, and Judge Robert Bork - original intent's leading conservative proponents - have misunderstood its meaning. The Framers, and Abraham Lincoln, their greatest proponent, believed that the Constitution was anchored in the principles of natural law invoked by the Declaration of Independence. Rehnquist and Bork are moral relativists and legal positivists, says Professor Jaffa, who repudiate the very existence of natural law and deny that the Declaration of Independence has any role whatsoever in constitutional interpretation. Nearly all the great constitutional controversies of our time have swirled around the meaning of the \"due process\" and \"equal protection\" clauses of the 14th Amendment.","publicationYear":1993,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Constitutional history","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-original-intent-and-the-framers-of-the-constitution-a-disputed-question-harry-v-jaffa"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Constitutional law","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-original-intent-and-the-framers-of-the-constitution-a-disputed-question-harry-v-jaffa"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Interpretation and construction","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-original-intent-and-the-framers-of-the-constitution-a-disputed-question-harry-v-jaffa"}],"displaySummary":"The Framers, and Abraham Lincoln, their greatest proponent, believed that the Constitution was anchored in the principles of natural law invoked by the Declaration of Independence. Rehnquist and Bork are moral relativists and legal positivists, says Professor Jaffa, who repudiate the very existence of natural law and deny that the Declaration of Independence has any role whatsoever in constitutional interpretation. Nearly all the great constitutional controversies of our time have swirled around the meaning of the \"due process\" and \"equal protection\" clauses of the 14th Amendment.","primaryTopic":"Constitutional History","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","confidence":"medium","method":"evid